{{!-- Faith Received — Ask panel markup, shared between the standalone
     /the-faith-received/ask/ route (custom-faith-ask.hbs) and the "Ask"
     tab on /the-faith-received/search/ (custom-faith-search.hbs,
     [data-fs-panel="ask"]). assets/js/page/faith-ask.js binds to this
     markup with plain, unscoped document.querySelector() calls (see that
     file's own header) — it doesn't care which page it's on, only that
     the markup exists exactly once on whichever page loads it, which is
     true here since the two routes never render at the same time. This
     partial exists so there is exactly one copy of that markup to keep in
     sync with the JS, instead of two.

     Whichever page includes this partial must also load, at the bottom
     of its own template and in this relative order, feature-gate.js
     (gates the submit button below to paid members) then
     page/faith-ask.js (the DOM binding) — see custom-faith-ask.hbs's own
     script block for the working order; page-template scripts run before
     site.min.js, so both must be loaded this way rather than assumed
     from the boot bundle.

     SHAPE (2026-09 redesign). This is a workspace you step into, not a
     widget sitting on a marketing page: .ask-workspace breaks out of
     whichever container it was dropped into and runs the full width of
     the viewport with its own ground, so the page visibly opens into it.
     Inside, three parts, in this DOM order, which is also the reading
     order and the tab order at every width:

       1. .ask-thread   — the transcript. Empty state, then the streaming
                          status line, then the answer, then errors. One
                          calm reading column, capped at a ~68ch measure.
       2. .ask-sources  — the citations. A sticky rail beside the thread
                          from 1025px up; stacked under it below that.
       3. .ask-dock     — the composer, at the bottom of the window and
                          the last thing in it. In flow, not pinned to
                          the viewport; the CSS block says why, and what
                          would change that.

     Multi-turn conversation is NOT built server-side yet (one question,
     one answer, per POST /v1/ask). Nothing here fakes it. But the shape
     is the one a follow-up drops into unchanged: when the worker grows a
     conversation, .ask-thread gains a sequence of turns above the dock
     and nothing else about this layout has to move.

     DOM contract faith-ask.js depends on — re-read that file before
     touching any of this:
       - form[data-ask-form] > textarea[data-ask-input],
         button[data-ask-submit] — the question box.
       - [data-ask-usage] wraps two rows. faith-ask.js finds the second
         (global-capacity) row via `usageMineRow.nextElementSibling`, NOT
         a data attribute — the [data-ask-usage-mine] row and the
         unlabeled row right after it must stay exactly adjacent siblings
         in that order.
       - [data-ask-status], [data-ask-result] containing
         [data-ask-heading], [data-ask-answer], [data-ask-gaps], and
         [data-ask-error] as a sibling of [data-ask-result].
       - [data-ask-footnotes-wrap] > [data-ask-footnotes], immediately
         followed by [data-ask-sources-empty] — the CSS rule
         ".ask-footnotes-wrap:not([hidden]) ~ .ask-sources-empty" in
         faith-received.css depends on that sibling order to hide the
         empty-state line once a real answer has citations.
       - button[data-feature-gate="ask"] on the submit button — the
         paid-member gate (assets/js/feature-gate.js intercepts the
         click). Do not remove or weaken this: any LLM-calling feature on
         this site is a standing member-gate + rate-limit requirement,
         and the worker itself enforces the same gate server-side (see
         faith-ask.js's header comment).
       - The scope control below ([data-ask-scope] and its
         [data-ask-tradition] / [data-ask-author] / [data-ask-scope-state]
         children) is read by faith-ask.js and sent as `traditions` and
         `author` in the POST body. The nine tradition values must stay
         byte-identical to KNOWN_TRADITIONS in
         website/workers/tfr-library/lib/ask.js — the worker drops any
         value it doesn't recognise, which would read as "the filter did
         nothing" rather than as an error.

     Deliberately NOT inside form[data-ask-form]: the scope control's
     author text input. A single-line text input inside a form triggers
     implicit submission on Enter, and implicit submission fires the
     form's submit event WITHOUT a click on [data-ask-submit] — which is
     exactly what feature-gate.js listens for. Keeping it out of the form
     means the only route to a question is the gated button. --}}
<div class="ask-workspace" data-ask-workspace>

  <div class="ask-workspace-inner">

    <div class="ask-thread">

      {{!-- Empty state. Hidden by CSS (a :has() rule on .ask-thread) the
            moment a status line, an answer, or an error appears, so it
            needs no JS of its own. The example questions only fill the
            question box; they never submit. A question costs real money
            and the paid-member gate lives on the submit button's own
            click, so nothing here may bypass it. --}}
      <div class="ask-empty">
        <p class="ask-empty-lede">A question, answered from the library itself.</p>
        <p class="ask-empty-note">Nineteen thousand works of Christian theology are open in front of you. Every claim in the answer carries a citation you can open in the reader.</p>
        <ul class="ask-examples">
          <li><button type="button" class="ask-example" data-ask-example>What does the tradition teach about justification?</button></li>
          <li><button type="button" class="ask-example" data-ask-example>Where do the Fathers and the Reformers divide on the Lord&rsquo;s Supper?</button></li>
          <li><button type="button" class="ask-example" data-ask-example>How did Augustine read the seventh chapter of Romans?</button></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="ask-status" data-ask-status role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>

      <article class="ask-result" data-ask-result hidden>
        <h2 class="ask-question" data-ask-heading></h2>
        <div class="ask-answer" data-ask-answer></div>

        <p class="ask-gaps" data-ask-gaps hidden></p>
      </article>

      <p class="ask-error" data-ask-error role="alert" hidden></p>

    </div>

    {{!-- Citations rail. .ask-footnotes-wrap/.ask-footnotes are exactly
         the elements faith-ask.js already renders into
         (renderFootnotes() in assets/js/page/faith-ask.js).
         .ask-sources-empty is pure CSS: a sibling selector hides it once
         .ask-footnotes-wrap loses its `hidden` attribute, so the rail
         never sits blank before a first question is asked. Below 1025px
         the whole rail is hidden until there is something in it, so a
         phone doesn't open onto a "Sources" heading with nothing under
         it. --}}
    <aside class="ask-sources" aria-label="Sources for this answer">
      <div class="ask-sources-inner">
        <h3 class="ask-footnotes-title">Sources</h3>
        <div class="ask-footnotes-wrap" data-ask-footnotes-wrap hidden>
          <ol class="ask-footnotes" data-ask-footnotes></ol>
        </div>
        <p class="ask-sources-empty" data-ask-sources-empty>Every page the answer draws on lists here, each one a link into the reader.</p>
      </div>
    </aside>

  </div>

  <div class="ask-dock">
    <div class="ask-dock-inner">
      <div class="ask-composer">

        <form class="ask-form" data-ask-form>
          <label class="visually-hidden" for="ask-question">Your question</label>
          <textarea
            id="ask-question"
            class="ask-input"
            data-ask-input
            rows="3"
            maxlength="600"
            placeholder="Ask the library a question&hellip;"
            required
          ></textarea>
          <div class="ask-form-footer">
            <p class="ask-form-hint">Enter to ask, Shift+Enter for a new line</p>
            <button type="submit" class="ask-submit" data-feature-gate="ask" data-ask-submit>
              Ask the library
            </button>
          </div>
        </form>

        {{!-- Scope. Collapsed by default and quiet on purpose: the whole
             library is the right default, and a reader who wants less
             than that goes looking for the control rather than being
             confronted with it. The nine traditions are the corpus's own
             vocabulary; see the DOM-contract note above. --}}
        <details class="ask-scope" data-ask-scope>
          <summary class="ask-scope-summary">
            <span class="ask-scope-title">Scope</span>
            <span class="ask-scope-state" data-ask-scope-state>The whole library</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="ask-scope-body">
            <fieldset class="ask-scope-field">
              <legend class="ask-scope-legend">Traditions</legend>
              <div class="ask-scope-pills">
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="English Divines" /><span>English Divines</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Greek Fathers" /><span>Greek Fathers</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Humanism and Law" /><span>Humanism and Law</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Lutheran" /><span>Lutheran</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Medieval" /><span>Medieval</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Latin Fathers" /><span>Latin Fathers</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Eastern Fathers" /><span>Eastern Fathers</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Roman Catholic" /><span>Roman Catholic</span></label>
                <label class="ask-scope-pill"><input type="checkbox" data-ask-tradition value="Reformed" /><span>Reformed</span></label>
              </div>
            </fieldset>
            <div class="ask-scope-field">
              <label class="ask-scope-legend" for="ask-scope-author">Author</label>
              <input
                id="ask-scope-author"
                class="ask-scope-input"
                type="text"
                data-ask-author
                maxlength="100"
                autocomplete="off"
                placeholder="e.g. Baxter"
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        {{!-- Usage meter -- a beta capacity cap on /v1/ask. Reads GET
              /v1/ask/usage on page load and after each answer -- see
              assets/js/page/faith-ask.js. Hidden until that first read
              succeeds, so a fetch failure degrades to "no meter shown"
              rather than a stuck "Loading…" line. --}}
        <div class="ask-usage" data-ask-usage hidden>
          <div class="ask-usage-row" data-ask-usage-mine hidden>
            <div class="ask-usage-meta">
              <span class="ask-usage-label">Your questions this month</span>
              <span class="ask-usage-pct" data-ask-usage-mine-text></span>
            </div>
            <div class="ask-usage-bar"><span class="ask-usage-fill" data-ask-usage-mine-fill></span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="ask-usage-row">
            <div class="ask-usage-meta">
              <span class="ask-usage-label">Beta capacity today</span>
              <span class="ask-usage-pct" data-ask-usage-global-text></span>
            </div>
            <div class="ask-usage-bar"><span class="ask-usage-fill" data-ask-usage-global-fill></span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
